e RAM `5.60–5.72 GB` at every launch.<br>**Prompt tok/s** — *mixed*: off `898.11` median (range `791.62–908.42`), mode 1 `310.65` (`305.96–324.09`, **−65.4%**, DISJOINT), **dual `958.96`** (`841.63–971.88`). *all-Q2_0*: off `905.25` (`856.82–920.92`), mode 1 `178.25` (`174.57–189.37`, **−80.3%**, DISJOINT), **dual `963.51`** (`867.81–989.27`).<br>**Decode tok/s** — *mixed*: off `40.30` (`38.36–41.60`), mode 1 `49.53` (**+22.9%**, DISJOINT), **dual `49.16` (+22.0%, DISJOINT)**. *all-Q2_0*: off `40.95` (`37.52–41.71`), mode 1 `50.95` (**+24.4%**, DISJOINT), **dual `50.65` (+23.7%, DISJOINT)**.<br>**The prompt gain is NOT claimed.** Dual's `+6.8%`/`+6.4%` over baseline has OVERLAPPING ranges and is reported as noise; the result is that dual **does not lose** prefill, not that it gains any. Every decode figure above has non-overlapping ranges and a gap wider than the largest within-arm spread.<br>**Cost, printed not estimated:** the shadow is `4.68 / 84.16 / 95.98 MiB` on the 4-, 72- and 168-tensor models — byte-for-byte what mode 1 places in `CPU_REPACK`, which independently confirms both modes select the same tensor set. It sits outside every ggml buffer and is therefore invisible to llama.cpp's `load_tensors:` totals, so it logs its own line (`CPU_REPACK_DUAL shadow buffer size = …`); at INFO on the first shadow and every 64 MiB, per tensor at DEBUG. The last INFO line understates the settled total by at most 64 MiB — the exact figure was deliberately not put behind a counter on the decode path (see the regression note below).<br>**Precision: unchanged, and tested where it is testable.** With `--no-op-offload`, which pins mode 2's prefill to the CPU exactly as mode 1's is and isolates the shadow from where prefill ran, mode 2 is **bit-identical to mode 1** on all six model × prompt cells: tokens identical, max `|Δ logprob| = 0.000000000`, deterministic 3/3. Under normal offload mode 2 stays bit-identical to mode 1 on any prompt below the batch-32 offload threshold, its graph splits track **mode 0** (`335`) on all three models and never mode 1 (`339/312/286`), and its divergence from mode 0 is never larger than mode 1's over the common prefix. On the 4-tensor control model all three modes are token-identical.<br>**Placement was measured before the code was written**, not inferred: `GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=2` shows `165/169` prefill `MUL_MAT`s on Vulkan0 with the weight in a plain CPU buffer versus `0/169` with it in `CPU_REPACK`, and `169/169` decode `MUL_MAT`s on the CPU in both.<br>**Recorded regression, because it is the useful part:** the first working version measured **slower than doing nothing** (decode `40.9 → 24.7`) while passing that entire correctness sweep. `get_tensor_traits` runs once per matmul **per worker thread** (~1.4k/token at 8 threads) and it carried two `getenv()`s, `strstr()`s over the tensor name, an **exclusive** mutex serialising all workers, and an unconditional seq_cst atomic. Fixed by resolving once at build time into `tensor->extra`. A green correctness sweep says nothing about the cost of the machinery that makes it correct.<br>**UNEXERCISED, declared:** `Q1_0` (no such model on disk — a pre-existing gap of this whole slot), in-process model reload, a run without `--no-host`, discrete GPUs, and Arm/NEON. Evidence: `research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/dual-residency/`. |
